{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U2"],
      "pins": ["U2.1", "U2.3"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VCC"],
      "observed": "On page 2 the LM1117-5.0 regulator U2 has pin 1 (VI, input) and pin 3 (VO, output) both tied to the same +5V net (two '+5V' arrows feed VI and VO). The actual power-input rail VCC (from J7 PWR pin 2) is not connected to the regulator input; VCC and +5V remain separate nets with no path between them.",
      "expected": "A linear regulator's input must be the higher unregulated rail and its output the regulated rail, and the two must not be shorted. VI should sit on VCC (the unregulated input that also feeds the fan via Q3) and VO on +5V, so the LDO steps VCC down to the 5 V that powers U1/U3. The spec does not describe this board's internals, so this is judged by standard practice and by the part's own function (LM1117-5.0 = 5 V regulator).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 2, U2 LM1117-5.0: '+5V' arrow -> pin1 VI ... pin3 VO -> '+5V' arrow (VI and VO on the same +5V net)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 1, J7 PWR pin2 -> VCC; VCC net = {J7.2, R5.1, C2.1, JP2.2} while +5V net = {U1.8, U3.8, U2.1, U2.3, R6.1, C3.1, J8.2, J3.1}; no wire joins VCC to +5V except through the mis-wired regulator"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 5 V MCU rail is never derived from the power input: with VI shorted to VO, the regulator does nothing and VCC cannot reach +5V, so the ATtiny(s) are unpowered when fed from J7; conversely if VCC were higher than 5 V, tying VI to the 5 V output would also fail to regulate the fan-side rail. Either way the board does not behave as a powered fan controller.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing is internally consistent except for the LM1117-5.0 regulator U2, whose input (VI, pin 1) and output (VO, pin 3) are both tied to the +5V net, leaving the VCC power-input rail unconnected to the regulator; this bridges the regulator's input and output nets and prevents the 5 V MCU rail from being powered, so an anomaly is reported."
}

